1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,880
October 1918.

2
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:05,198
In the Meuse-Argonne, in northeastern France, the

3
00:00:05,198 --> 00:00:08,313
American army is fighting its greatest battle.

4
00:00:08,313 --> 00:00:11,563
Over 47 days, more than a million Americans will

5
00:00:11,563 --> 00:00:12,240
take part.

6
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:16,489
By the time it ends, more than 26,000 of them will

7
00:00:16,489 --> 00:00:17,097
be dead.

8
00:00:17,097 --> 00:00:20,439
The Library of Congress calls it the highest

9
00:00:20,439 --> 00:00:24,769
American death toll of any single battle in U.S. history.

10
00:00:25,219 --> 00:00:28,080
But for every American killed in battle in this war,

11
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:29,676
about four more were wounded.

12
00:00:30,126 --> 00:00:32,073
So imagine you are one of them.

13
00:00:32,073 --> 00:00:35,340
You are an infantryman in the American Expeditionary

14
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:35,780
Forces.

15
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:37,162
A shell bursts nearby.

16
00:00:37,162 --> 00:00:38,355
Something hits you.

17
00:00:38,805 --> 00:00:40,140
You have no antibiotics.

18
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:42,531
They will not exist for another generation.

19
00:00:42,531 --> 00:00:45,311
Blood transfusion is brand new on the battlefield.

20
00:00:45,311 --> 00:00:48,425
And the nearest surgeon may be a dozen miles behind you.

21
00:00:48,875 --> 00:00:51,623
Doctors knew they had about 12 hours to operate

22
00:00:51,623 --> 00:00:53,552
before infection could take hold.

23
00:00:53,552 --> 00:00:56,066
In a big battle, the roads were jammed, the

24
00:00:56,066 --> 00:00:58,873
stretcher bearers waited for dark, and the clock

25
00:00:58,873 --> 00:00:59,633
kept running.

26
00:01:00,083 --> 00:01:03,051
This is what happened to a wounded American in the

27
00:01:03,051 --> 00:01:04,000
First World War.

28
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,027
Step by step, from the moment he was hit to the day

29
00:01:07,027 --> 00:01:09,520
he came home, from the Army's own records.

30
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:10,665
Wounded.

31
00:01:10,665 --> 00:01:12,339
Nineteen eighteen.

32
00:01:14,739 --> 00:01:16,123
It began with doctors.

33
00:01:17,723 --> 00:01:20,647
Long before most American combat troops reached

34
00:01:20,647 --> 00:01:23,758
France, American medical units were already there.

35
00:01:24,208 --> 00:01:25,420
Base Hospital No.

36
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:29,414
4, the Lakeside Unit from Cleveland, sailed in May 1917.

37
00:01:29,414 --> 00:01:33,122
Its own city's history calls it the first contingent

38
00:01:33,122 --> 00:01:36,830
of the American Expeditionary Forces sent to Europe.

39
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:40,317
It was one of six hospital units rushed to France to

40
00:01:40,317 --> 00:01:43,237
help the British, who had been fighting for nearly

41
00:01:43,237 --> 00:01:43,938
three years.

42
00:01:44,388 --> 00:01:47,379
The first combat casualty of the American

43
00:01:47,379 --> 00:01:50,808
Expeditionary Forces was a medical officer too:

44
00:01:50,808 --> 00:01:54,602
Captain Louis Genella, of the Medical Reserve Corps,

45
00:01:54,602 --> 00:01:57,885
hit by artillery near Arras on July 14, 1917.

46
00:01:58,335 --> 00:01:59,513
And the first U.S.

47
00:01:59,513 --> 00:02:03,177
Army soldiers killed by enemy action were at a hospital.

48
00:02:03,627 --> 00:02:07,162
On the night of September 4, 1917, German bombers

49
00:02:07,162 --> 00:02:08,676
hit Base Hospital No.

50
00:02:08,676 --> 00:02:12,283
5, Harvard's unit, at Camiers on the French coast.

51
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:16,101
Lieutenant William Fitzsimons and three privates,

52
00:02:16,101 --> 00:02:20,019
Oscar Tugo, Rudolph Rubino and Leslie Woods, were killed.

53
00:02:20,019 --> 00:02:23,250
The Army's medical history calls them the first

54
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:26,549
deaths of the American Expeditionary Forces from

55
00:02:26,549 --> 00:02:27,443
enemy action.

56
00:02:27,893 --> 00:02:31,285
Harvard's unit had reached France less than a month

57
00:02:31,285 --> 00:02:33,281
after its call for volunteers.

58
00:02:33,281 --> 00:02:36,540
Over the war, it treated more than 45,000 British

59
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:38,004
and American patients.

60
00:02:38,454 --> 00:02:41,230
By the time the war ended, the Army Medical

61
00:02:41,230 --> 00:02:44,136
Department had grown into an army of its own.

62
00:02:44,586 --> 00:02:47,640
By November 1918, almost two million American

63
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:49,269
soldiers were in Europe.

64
00:02:49,269 --> 00:02:52,120
Every one of them was a potential patient.

65
00:02:52,570 --> 00:02:56,942
Overseas, it had more than 18,000 officers, about

66
00:02:56,942 --> 00:03:01,047
10,000 nurses and nearly 146,000 enlisted men.

67
00:03:01,497 --> 00:03:04,198
Its job was to take a man from the moment he was

68
00:03:04,198 --> 00:03:06,842
hit, through a chain of stations that stretched

69
00:03:06,842 --> 00:03:09,599
hundreds of miles, all the way back to the United

70
00:03:09,599 --> 00:03:10,724
States if it had to.

71
00:03:11,174 --> 00:03:14,542
At home, the Army's hospital system grew just as fast.

72
00:03:14,542 --> 00:03:17,536
Its hospital beds in the United States went from

73
00:03:17,536 --> 00:03:20,593
about ninety-five hundred to a hundred and twenty

74
00:03:20,593 --> 00:03:21,154
thousand.

75
00:03:21,604 --> 00:03:25,080
The United States was in the war for about 19 months.

76
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:28,294
The National Park Service notes its battle deaths

77
00:03:28,294 --> 00:03:30,983
came in less than six months of fighting.

78
00:03:30,983 --> 00:03:34,328
About fifty-three thousand Americans were killed in

79
00:03:34,328 --> 00:03:34,787
battle.

80
00:03:34,787 --> 00:03:36,820
More than 200,000 were wounded.

81
00:03:37,270 --> 00:03:40,545
A statistician for the War Department, Leonard

82
00:03:40,545 --> 00:03:42,539
Ayres, summed it up in 1919.

83
00:03:42,539 --> 00:03:46,028
For every man killed in battle, seven others were

84
00:03:46,028 --> 00:03:49,232
wounded, taken prisoner, or reported missing.

85
00:03:49,682 --> 00:03:52,216
This is the story of the 200,000.

86
00:03:52,816 --> 00:03:54,152
So what actually hit them?

87
00:03:55,752 --> 00:03:57,162
Not what you might expect.

88
00:03:57,162 --> 00:03:59,279
Not the bayonet charge from the movies.

89
00:03:59,728 --> 00:04:02,559
The Army counted every wounded man who reached a

90
00:04:02,559 --> 00:04:04,622
hospital, and what had wounded him.

91
00:04:04,622 --> 00:04:07,452
A study for the Army's Command and General Staff

92
00:04:07,452 --> 00:04:09,457
College later reprinted the table.

93
00:04:09,907 --> 00:04:12,640
Bayonets? 235 cases.

94
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,962
About 1 in 1,000.

95
00:04:15,412 --> 00:04:17,385
Rifle bullets: about 9%.

96
00:04:17,835 --> 00:04:20,858
Shrapnel and shell fragments: about 23%.

97
00:04:21,308 --> 00:04:24,882
And the single biggest named cause of all? Gas.

98
00:04:24,882 --> 00:04:26,022
Almost a third.

99
00:04:26,472 --> 00:04:29,291
Another third were listed only as gun wounds, with

100
00:04:29,291 --> 00:04:30,193
no weapon named.

101
00:04:30,193 --> 00:04:32,053
Other counts fill in the picture.

102
00:04:32,053 --> 00:04:33,519
This was an artillery war.

103
00:04:33,969 --> 00:04:37,532
The same table shows who died after reaching a hospital.

104
00:04:37,532 --> 00:04:38,805
Gas filled the beds.

105
00:04:38,805 --> 00:04:42,177
But shell fragments and gun wounds filled the graves:

106
00:04:42,177 --> 00:04:45,358
thousands of deaths from fragments and gun wounds,

107
00:04:45,358 --> 00:04:47,204
against about 1,200 from gas.

108
00:04:47,654 --> 00:04:50,675
The National World War I Museum estimates that

109
00:04:50,675 --> 00:04:54,025
across all the armies, about six in ten battlefield

110
00:04:54,025 --> 00:04:57,506
casualties were caused by exploding artillery shells.

111
00:04:57,956 --> 00:05:01,187
An American sergeant, Donald Kyler, wrote that shell

112
00:05:01,187 --> 00:05:04,355
fragment wounds were the most numerous and the most

113
00:05:04,355 --> 00:05:06,592
serious of any his company suffered.

114
00:05:07,042 --> 00:05:08,849
A shell fragment is not clean.

115
00:05:08,849 --> 00:05:11,680
It is jagged and hot, and it drives whatever it

116
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:13,487
passes through into the wound:

117
00:05:13,487 --> 00:05:15,475
cloth from the uniform, and dirt.

118
00:05:15,925 --> 00:05:18,893
And the dirt of northern France had been farmed and

119
00:05:18,893 --> 00:05:21,046
fertilized with manure for centuries.

120
00:05:21,046 --> 00:05:23,897
It was full of bacteria that live without oxygen.

121
00:05:24,347 --> 00:05:27,259
Driven deep into torn muscle, some of them caused

122
00:05:27,259 --> 00:05:30,290
the most feared infection of the war: gas gangrene.

123
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:34,184
One study at a hospital in France followed more than

124
00:05:34,184 --> 00:05:37,428
2,000 two hundred wounded French soldiers in 1915

125
00:05:37,428 --> 00:05:38,223
and sixteen.

126
00:05:38,223 --> 00:05:40,673
A hundred and seven got gas gangrene.

127
00:05:40,673 --> 00:05:43,653
Twenty-five of them died, almost one in four.

128
00:05:44,103 --> 00:05:45,019
It spread fast.

129
00:05:45,019 --> 00:05:48,315
That is why the Army's surgeons talked about 12 hours.

130
00:05:48,315 --> 00:05:51,306
If a wound could be cleaned out surgically within

131
00:05:51,306 --> 00:05:54,358
about 12 hours, the man's chances were far better.

132
00:05:54,808 --> 00:05:55,762
Twelve hours.

133
00:05:55,762 --> 00:05:57,303
Remember that number.

134
00:05:57,303 --> 00:06:00,972
Everything that happens next is a race against it.

135
00:06:01,572 --> 00:06:03,302
The race starts in your own pocket.

136
00:06:04,902 --> 00:06:08,419
Every American soldier carried a first-aid packet. Inside:

137
00:06:08,419 --> 00:06:11,391
two gauze bandages, two small compresses, and two

138
00:06:11,391 --> 00:06:12,119
safety pins.

139
00:06:12,569 --> 00:06:16,009
The battalion surgeon taught every man how to put it on.

140
00:06:16,009 --> 00:06:17,238
The idea was simple:

141
00:06:17,238 --> 00:06:20,187
cover the wound and protect it from more damage,

142
00:06:20,187 --> 00:06:21,354
until help arrived.

143
00:06:21,804 --> 00:06:23,706
Help meant the company aid men.

144
00:06:23,706 --> 00:06:26,713
Each infantry company had two enlisted men of the

145
00:06:26,713 --> 00:06:27,879
Medical Department.

146
00:06:27,879 --> 00:06:29,167
Eight to a battalion.

147
00:06:29,617 --> 00:06:32,439
Their job was to find the wounded, mark where they

148
00:06:32,439 --> 00:06:33,624
lay, and gather them.

149
00:06:33,624 --> 00:06:36,446
About four in ten of the wounded could still walk,

150
00:06:36,446 --> 00:06:38,308
and they made their own way back.

151
00:06:38,758 --> 00:06:40,316
Everyone else had to be carried.

152
00:06:40,766 --> 00:06:43,062
That was the work of the stretcher bearers.

153
00:06:43,062 --> 00:06:45,677
On the Western Front, they trained for ten weeks.

154
00:06:45,677 --> 00:06:47,332
They worked in teams of 4 to 6.

155
00:06:47,332 --> 00:06:48,933
And they went forward unarmed.

156
00:06:49,383 --> 00:06:51,495
The mud was sometimes up to their knees.

157
00:06:51,495 --> 00:06:53,977
The wooden handles of the stretchers rotted and

158
00:06:53,977 --> 00:06:56,617
splintered, and the bearers' hands were covered in

159
00:06:56,617 --> 00:06:57,832
blisters and splinters.

160
00:06:58,282 --> 00:07:01,191
They drew their own maps of the trenches, and redrew

161
00:07:01,191 --> 00:07:03,822
them as the trenches collapsed under shellfire.

162
00:07:04,272 --> 00:07:07,102
They were not armed, and they were not protected.

163
00:07:07,102 --> 00:07:09,644
Their only shield was the Red Cross on their

164
00:07:09,644 --> 00:07:12,532
armband, and in an artillery war, a shell does not

165
00:07:12,532 --> 00:07:13,341
read armbands.

166
00:07:13,791 --> 00:07:17,044
And the hardest part of the job, as one history of

167
00:07:17,044 --> 00:07:18,540
them puts it, was this:

168
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:21,728
to carry a man through enemy fire, and find, when

169
00:07:21,728 --> 00:07:24,915
they finally reached the aid station, that he was

170
00:07:24,915 --> 00:07:25,761
already dead.

171
00:07:26,211 --> 00:07:29,107
Under fire, the carry back often had to wait for night.

172
00:07:29,557 --> 00:07:32,352
So the first hours of your twelve could be spent

173
00:07:32,352 --> 00:07:35,205
lying where you fell, or in a shell hole, waiting

174
00:07:35,205 --> 00:07:35,787
for night.

175
00:07:36,237 --> 00:07:39,070
Then the bearers came, and you were carried to the

176
00:07:39,070 --> 00:07:40,713
first real stop on the chain:

177
00:07:40,713 --> 00:07:42,186
the battalion aid station.

178
00:07:42,636 --> 00:07:46,190
It sat about 500 to 1,000 yards behind the line, in

179
00:07:46,190 --> 00:07:49,396
a dugout, or in whatever building still stood.

180
00:07:49,846 --> 00:07:51,228
One medical officer.

181
00:07:51,228 --> 00:07:52,610
4 to 6 enlisted men.

182
00:07:52,610 --> 00:07:55,513
Two runners, and squads of litter bearers.

183
00:07:55,513 --> 00:07:57,379
Room for 12 to 30 patients.

184
00:07:57,829 --> 00:08:00,785
Here the doctor did what he could, and only that.

185
00:08:00,785 --> 00:08:02,233
Iodine around the wound.

186
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:03,258
A fresh dressing.

187
00:08:03,258 --> 00:08:05,129
Something to stop the bleeding.

188
00:08:05,129 --> 00:08:06,456
Morphine for the pain.

189
00:08:06,906 --> 00:08:09,810
Blankets, and a hot drink, because the doctors had

190
00:08:09,810 --> 00:08:12,773
learned that a wounded man who was cold and wet was

191
00:08:12,773 --> 00:08:14,457
more likely to go into shock.

192
00:08:14,907 --> 00:08:17,108
And a shot of tetanus antitoxin.

193
00:08:17,108 --> 00:08:18,896
Every wounded man got one.

194
00:08:19,346 --> 00:08:22,806
That single shot was one of the quiet successes of

195
00:08:22,806 --> 00:08:23,360
the war.

196
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:26,958
In the British Army in September 1914, before it was

197
00:08:26,958 --> 00:08:30,419
routine, about 9 of every 1,000 wounded men landed

198
00:08:30,419 --> 00:08:32,010
in England had tetanus.

199
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:35,697
Once every wounded man got the shot, the rate fell

200
00:08:35,697 --> 00:08:38,480
to about 1.5 per 1,000 within three months.

201
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:41,522
At American aid stations, the shot was routine.

202
00:08:41,972 --> 00:08:44,963
Then the doctor filled out a field medical card for you.

203
00:08:45,413 --> 00:08:47,820
There were eight categories, from very slightly

204
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:49,255
wounded, to gassed, to sick.

205
00:08:49,705 --> 00:08:52,453
But if the shell had broken your thigh bone, there

206
00:08:52,453 --> 00:08:54,872
was one more thing the aid station could do.

207
00:08:54,872 --> 00:08:57,566
And it was the difference between life and death.

208
00:08:58,166 --> 00:08:59,098
Four in five.

209
00:09:00,698 --> 00:09:03,786
Early in the war, a soldier whose thigh bone was

210
00:09:03,786 --> 00:09:07,003
shattered by a shell or a bullet, a so-called open

211
00:09:07,003 --> 00:09:09,512
femur fracture, was very likely to die.

212
00:09:09,962 --> 00:09:13,536
The British surgeon Henry Gray later put the death

213
00:09:13,536 --> 00:09:15,966
rate at roughly 80%. Four in five.

214
00:09:16,416 --> 00:09:19,054
The femur, the thigh bone, is the longest bone in

215
00:09:19,054 --> 00:09:19,538
the body.

216
00:09:19,538 --> 00:09:21,745
Shattered, it is very hard to hold still.

217
00:09:22,195 --> 00:09:24,890
And without support, every jolt of a stretcher,

218
00:09:24,890 --> 00:09:27,814
across shell holes and duckboards, moved the broken

219
00:09:27,814 --> 00:09:28,445
bone again.

220
00:09:28,895 --> 00:09:30,967
The answer was an old invention.

221
00:09:30,967 --> 00:09:34,269
A frame of iron with a padded ring at the top and a

222
00:09:34,269 --> 00:09:37,312
bar at the bottom, designed in Liverpool in the

223
00:09:37,312 --> 00:09:41,067
1870s by a surgeon and bone-setter named Hugh Owen Thomas.

224
00:09:41,517 --> 00:09:43,156
The ring sat against the hip.

225
00:09:43,156 --> 00:09:45,020
The foot was tied to the far end.

226
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:47,676
Then the leg was pulled straight and held under

227
00:09:47,676 --> 00:09:50,557
tension, so the broken ends could not grind against

228
00:09:50,557 --> 00:09:51,179
each other.

229
00:09:51,629 --> 00:09:55,099
Thomas's nephew, the surgeon Robert Jones, pushed

230
00:09:55,099 --> 00:09:57,932
the British Army to use it at the front.

231
00:09:57,932 --> 00:09:59,915
He recommended it from 1914.

232
00:09:59,915 --> 00:10:01,615
By 1917 it was standard.

233
00:10:02,065 --> 00:10:05,234
Putting it on correctly took twelve stages, ideally

234
00:10:05,234 --> 00:10:06,414
by a team of three.

235
00:10:06,864 --> 00:10:09,686
One British surgical history describes bearers

236
00:10:09,686 --> 00:10:12,877
crawling out into no-man's-land at night to splint a

237
00:10:12,877 --> 00:10:14,778
wounded man's leg where he lay.

238
00:10:15,228 --> 00:10:16,788
Then Gray counted.

239
00:10:16,788 --> 00:10:21,120
At Arras, in the spring of 1917, he followed 1,009

240
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:23,286
men with these fractures.

241
00:10:23,286 --> 00:10:25,192
About one in six died.

242
00:10:25,642 --> 00:10:27,998
From four in five, to about one in six.

243
00:10:27,998 --> 00:10:30,958
Historians warn that faster ambulances and better

244
00:10:30,958 --> 00:10:32,227
hospitals helped too.

245
00:10:32,227 --> 00:10:35,731
But the splint is still credited with much of that change.

246
00:10:36,181 --> 00:10:39,105
When the Americans came, the Thomas splint was part

247
00:10:39,105 --> 00:10:42,086
of the standard kit at their battalion aid stations.

248
00:10:42,536 --> 00:10:45,850
A piece of iron from the 1870s turned out to be one

249
00:10:45,850 --> 00:10:48,125
of the great lifesavers of the war.

250
00:10:48,575 --> 00:10:50,425
Now you had to get to a surgeon.

251
00:10:50,425 --> 00:10:53,430
And between you and him were some of the most jammed

252
00:10:53,430 --> 00:10:54,413
roads of the war.

253
00:10:55,013 --> 00:10:55,905
The road back.

254
00:10:57,505 --> 00:11:00,852
From the aid station, the bearers carried you back

255
00:11:00,852 --> 00:11:03,261
to where the ambulances could reach.

256
00:11:03,261 --> 00:11:06,742
Behind every American division was a unit called the

257
00:11:06,742 --> 00:11:08,750
sanitary train, about 950 men.

258
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:12,802
It had one horse-drawn ambulance company and three

259
00:11:12,802 --> 00:11:14,675
motor ambulance companies:

260
00:11:14,675 --> 00:11:18,638
twelve animal-drawn ambulances and 36 motor ambulances.

261
00:11:19,088 --> 00:11:21,424
The horse company was usually held back.

262
00:11:21,424 --> 00:11:24,404
Horses were slow, they tired quickly, and they made

263
00:11:24,404 --> 00:11:25,280
a large target.

264
00:11:25,730 --> 00:11:28,419
Often, the ambulance that came for you was a Ford.

265
00:11:28,869 --> 00:11:30,218
The Model T ambulance.

266
00:11:30,218 --> 00:11:33,284
A 20-horsepower engine, started with a hand crank.

267
00:11:33,284 --> 00:11:36,472
It carried three stretchers, or four men sitting up.

268
00:11:36,922 --> 00:11:40,358
It was light enough that when it got stuck in a

269
00:11:40,358 --> 00:11:43,721
hole, a few soldiers could simply lift it out.

270
00:11:43,721 --> 00:11:47,449
By November 1, 1918, 4,362 of them had been shipped

271
00:11:47,449 --> 00:11:48,107
overseas.

272
00:11:48,557 --> 00:11:51,801
The other common Army ambulance was the heavier White.

273
00:11:51,801 --> 00:11:54,805
It carried four stretcher patients, a driver and a

274
00:11:54,805 --> 00:11:57,027
medic, at about twenty miles an hour.

275
00:11:57,477 --> 00:12:00,489
This film shows American volunteer drivers serving

276
00:12:00,489 --> 00:12:03,260
with the French Army, carrying French wounded.

277
00:12:03,260 --> 00:12:06,512
Their ambulances and their roads looked much the same.

278
00:12:06,962 --> 00:12:07,905
Now, the drive.

279
00:12:07,905 --> 00:12:09,790
On paper, it was a short trip.

280
00:12:09,790 --> 00:12:12,680
The advanced dressing station sat about a mile

281
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:13,748
behind the front.

282
00:12:13,748 --> 00:12:16,198
The field hospitals, 6 to 8 miles back.

283
00:12:16,648 --> 00:12:19,170
First stop, the advanced dressing station:

284
00:12:19,170 --> 00:12:22,173
one officer and fifteen men, 1,500 to two thousand

285
00:12:22,173 --> 00:12:23,434
yards from the front.

286
00:12:23,434 --> 00:12:26,376
Then the main dressing station, up to three and a

287
00:12:26,376 --> 00:12:27,337
half miles back.

288
00:12:27,787 --> 00:12:30,716
At the start of the Argonne drive, the evacuation

289
00:12:30,716 --> 00:12:33,765
hospitals, where most of the real surgery happened,

290
00:12:33,765 --> 00:12:36,634
were thirty to sixty kilometers behind the field

291
00:12:36,634 --> 00:12:37,232
hospitals.

292
00:12:37,682 --> 00:12:39,808
In practice, the roads were the problem.

293
00:12:40,258 --> 00:12:43,218
Every road behind the front carried everything at once.

294
00:12:43,218 --> 00:12:44,294
Ammunition going up.

295
00:12:44,294 --> 00:12:45,048
Food going up.

296
00:12:45,048 --> 00:12:47,524
Whole divisions moving in and out of the line.

297
00:12:47,524 --> 00:12:49,676
And the ambulances, trying to come back.

298
00:12:50,126 --> 00:12:53,076
The Army's nursing history describes roads

299
00:12:53,076 --> 00:12:56,308
constantly jammed, and fire that often made it

300
00:12:56,308 --> 00:12:59,398
impossible to remove the wounded until dark.

301
00:12:59,848 --> 00:13:02,486
In the Argonne, some roads were simply impassable.

302
00:13:02,486 --> 00:13:05,018
The walking wounded walked, because no ambulance

303
00:13:05,018 --> 00:13:05,968
could get to them.

304
00:13:06,418 --> 00:13:09,482
In October, ambulances bogged down in the mud of the

305
00:13:09,482 --> 00:13:09,835
Meuse.

306
00:13:09,835 --> 00:13:13,018
Engineers worked day after day to keep the roads open.

307
00:13:13,468 --> 00:13:16,615
By the Army's own count, ambulances in the

308
00:13:16,615 --> 00:13:20,288
Meuse-Argonne drove more than 900,000 kilometers,

309
00:13:20,288 --> 00:13:24,110
and carried more than 132,000 sick and wounded men.

310
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:27,853
On average, a wounded man reached a field hospital

311
00:13:27,853 --> 00:13:31,080
somewhere between 3 and 8 hours after he was hit.

312
00:13:31,530 --> 00:13:33,296
On good days it was faster.

313
00:13:33,296 --> 00:13:36,565
At Soissons in July, the wounded were cleared from

314
00:13:36,565 --> 00:13:39,180
the battlefield within six hours. At St.

315
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:42,449
Mihiel, some reached an evacuation hospital within

316
00:13:42,449 --> 00:13:44,084
three hours of being hit.

317
00:13:44,534 --> 00:13:46,279
On bad days, it fell apart.

318
00:13:46,279 --> 00:13:49,576
In early June 1918, more than seven hundred wounded

319
00:13:49,576 --> 00:13:52,937
men reached the American Red Cross military hospital

320
00:13:52,937 --> 00:13:53,519
in Paris.

321
00:13:53,519 --> 00:13:55,716
Many had not yet been operated on.

322
00:13:55,716 --> 00:13:57,332
Many had died on the way.

323
00:13:57,782 --> 00:13:58,737
3 to 8 hours.

324
00:13:58,737 --> 00:14:00,353
Gone from your twelve.

325
00:14:00,353 --> 00:14:03,070
And you still had not seen a surgeon.

326
00:14:03,670 --> 00:14:05,765
The French had a word for what happened next.

327
00:14:07,365 --> 00:14:07,889
Triage.

328
00:14:07,889 --> 00:14:10,586
From the French verb trier, to sort.

329
00:14:11,036 --> 00:14:14,752
Each American division had four field hospitals, set

330
00:14:14,752 --> 00:14:18,254
up about 6 to 8 miles behind the front, each with

331
00:14:18,254 --> 00:14:19,827
room for 216 patients.

332
00:14:19,827 --> 00:14:22,257
One of the four became the triage.

333
00:14:22,707 --> 00:14:26,085
Its job was to sort, classify and distribute the wounded.

334
00:14:26,085 --> 00:14:29,048
To decide, quickly, who needed what, and who could

335
00:14:29,048 --> 00:14:30,825
survive the trip further back.

336
00:14:31,275 --> 00:14:33,331
Here is how it worked at St.

337
00:14:33,331 --> 00:14:36,928
Mihiel, in September 1918, for the 82nd Division.

338
00:14:37,378 --> 00:14:40,724
Field Hospital 328, in the village of Dieulouard,

339
00:14:40,724 --> 00:14:41,748
was the triage.

340
00:14:41,748 --> 00:14:45,709
It began receiving wounded 8 hours after the attack began.

341
00:14:46,159 --> 00:14:48,902
Field Hospital 325 took the sick.

342
00:14:48,902 --> 00:14:50,564
326 took the gassed.

343
00:14:50,564 --> 00:14:53,141
327 took the seriously wounded.

344
00:14:53,591 --> 00:14:56,167
A man expected to recover in two weeks or less

345
00:14:56,167 --> 00:14:57,568
stayed with the division.

346
00:14:57,568 --> 00:15:00,089
Everyone else was prepared for the trip back.

347
00:15:00,539 --> 00:15:03,546
The Army also tried bringing the surgeon closer to

348
00:15:03,546 --> 00:15:04,267
the wounded.

349
00:15:04,267 --> 00:15:07,033
Mobile hospitals, copied from the French, were

350
00:15:07,033 --> 00:15:10,281
designed to deliver lifesaving surgery near the front.

351
00:15:10,731 --> 00:15:13,828
It was a good idea that came too late to matter much.

352
00:15:13,828 --> 00:15:16,633
By the end of the war, twelve of these units had

353
00:15:16,633 --> 00:15:19,788
treated only about one in a hundred wounded Americans.

354
00:15:20,238 --> 00:15:20,721
For St.

355
00:15:20,721 --> 00:15:24,173
Mihiel, the Army planned for thirty-three thousand

356
00:15:24,173 --> 00:15:27,417
casualties, with ten evacuation hospitals, five

357
00:15:27,417 --> 00:15:30,592
mobile hospitals and nine evacuation ambulance

358
00:15:30,592 --> 00:15:31,697
companies ready.

359
00:15:32,147 --> 00:15:34,497
Surgery here was not meant to be final.

360
00:15:34,497 --> 00:15:37,389
The mission, in the words of one history, was to

361
00:15:37,389 --> 00:15:40,402
first save lives, and then to prepare the patients

362
00:15:40,402 --> 00:15:42,089
for the evacuation hospital.

363
00:15:42,539 --> 00:15:46,372
At Cantigny, in May 1918, one field hospital, No.

364
00:15:46,372 --> 00:15:50,362
13, served as both the triage and the gas hospital.

365
00:15:50,812 --> 00:15:51,774
Stop the bleeding.

366
00:15:51,774 --> 00:15:53,110
Straighten the fractures.

367
00:15:53,110 --> 00:15:55,461
Clean out the wound, only if there was time.

368
00:15:55,911 --> 00:15:58,695
Because many of the men who reached the triage were

369
00:15:58,695 --> 00:15:59,624
not just wounded.

370
00:15:59,624 --> 00:16:00,661
They were in shock.

371
00:16:01,111 --> 00:16:04,242
Their circulation was failing, and no one yet fully

372
00:16:04,242 --> 00:16:05,163
understood why.

373
00:16:05,613 --> 00:16:09,172
In 1917, a British medical committee defined it as

374
00:16:09,172 --> 00:16:12,660
circulatory failure, because not enough blood was

375
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:14,012
reaching the heart.

376
00:16:14,462 --> 00:16:17,298
An American physiologist from Harvard, Walter

377
00:16:17,298 --> 00:16:20,765
Cannon, went to France to study it in wounded soldiers.

378
00:16:20,765 --> 00:16:23,979
He concluded the core problem was too little oxygen

379
00:16:23,979 --> 00:16:25,744
reaching the body's tissues.

380
00:16:26,194 --> 00:16:29,713
So the shock treatment at the triage was, above all,

381
00:16:29,713 --> 00:16:30,864
warmth and fluid.

382
00:16:30,864 --> 00:16:33,706
Wet clothes cut off. Blankets. Hot drinks.

383
00:16:33,706 --> 00:16:35,263
Salt water into a vein.

384
00:16:35,713 --> 00:16:39,040
In July 1918, the Army's surgical consultants sent a

385
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:41,920
dedicated shock team to Field Hospital Number

386
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:42,752
Twenty-Seven.

387
00:16:42,752 --> 00:16:45,375
It was a sign of where things were going:

388
00:16:45,375 --> 00:16:47,807
treat the shock first, then the wound.

389
00:16:48,257 --> 00:16:50,295
So far, then, the journey looked like this.

390
00:16:50,745 --> 00:16:53,673
And, if a team was there to do it, something new on

391
00:16:53,673 --> 00:16:54,592
the battlefield:

392
00:16:54,592 --> 00:16:56,142
blood from a matched donor.

393
00:16:56,742 --> 00:16:57,657
Blood on ice.

394
00:16:59,257 --> 00:17:01,661
A man in shock from bleeding needs blood.

395
00:17:01,661 --> 00:17:04,417
Saline can fill the vessels for a while, but it

396
00:17:04,417 --> 00:17:05,589
cannot carry oxygen.

397
00:17:06,039 --> 00:17:09,183
By 1918, three discoveries had come together.

398
00:17:09,183 --> 00:17:12,746
Around 1900, Karl Landsteiner in Vienna showed that

399
00:17:12,746 --> 00:17:15,331
human blood comes in different types.

400
00:17:15,781 --> 00:17:18,683
In 1914 and fifteen, doctors in Belgium, in

401
00:17:18,683 --> 00:17:22,193
Argentina and in New York found that a little sodium

402
00:17:22,193 --> 00:17:24,420
citrate kept blood from clotting.

403
00:17:24,870 --> 00:17:28,197
And in 1916, two American researchers, Peyton Rous

404
00:17:28,197 --> 00:17:31,524
and Joseph Turner, added sugar to the mix, so that

405
00:17:31,524 --> 00:17:32,988
blood could be stored.

406
00:17:33,438 --> 00:17:36,282
The man who put it all together at the front was a

407
00:17:36,282 --> 00:17:39,125
young American Army doctor named Oswald Robertson.

408
00:17:39,575 --> 00:17:41,438
He came with Base Hospital No.

409
00:17:41,438 --> 00:17:42,556
5, Harvard's unit.

410
00:17:42,556 --> 00:17:45,537
He collected blood from group O donors, the type

411
00:17:45,537 --> 00:17:48,704
that could be given to almost anyone, mixed it with

412
00:17:48,704 --> 00:17:51,809
citrate and sugar, sealed it in glass bottles, and

413
00:17:51,809 --> 00:17:52,865
packed it in ice.

414
00:17:53,315 --> 00:17:56,028
Blood kept that way could last up to 26 days.

415
00:17:56,028 --> 00:17:59,044
For the first time, blood could be waiting for the

416
00:17:59,044 --> 00:18:01,516
wounded, instead of the other way around.

417
00:18:01,966 --> 00:18:03,047
Then he used it.

418
00:18:03,047 --> 00:18:06,290
In what is often called the first blood bank, he

419
00:18:06,290 --> 00:18:09,736
gave 22 units of stored blood to twenty wounded men

420
00:18:09,736 --> 00:18:12,776
who were too badly hurt to withstand surgery.

421
00:18:13,226 --> 00:18:16,284
In a British medical journal in June 1918, he

422
00:18:16,284 --> 00:18:19,682
reported that the effect of stored blood was fully

423
00:18:19,682 --> 00:18:22,060
as striking as that of fresh blood.

424
00:18:22,510 --> 00:18:25,918
And he made the case for the future in one sentence.

425
00:18:25,918 --> 00:18:29,128
There is a definite need, he wrote, in front area

426
00:18:29,128 --> 00:18:32,929
medical work, of a method for giving transfusions rapidly.

427
00:18:33,379 --> 00:18:36,418
Blood pressure, he wrote, rose by 20 to 40 points.

428
00:18:36,418 --> 00:18:39,580
Britain awarded him its Distinguished Service Order.

429
00:18:40,030 --> 00:18:43,191
A Canadian doctor with a similar name, Lawrence

430
00:18:43,191 --> 00:18:45,949
Bruce Robertson, was already giving blood

431
00:18:45,949 --> 00:18:47,698
transfusions at the front.

432
00:18:47,698 --> 00:18:51,196
In men bleeding heavily and in shock, he reported an

433
00:18:51,196 --> 00:18:54,156
immediate and almost incredible improvement.

434
00:18:54,606 --> 00:18:56,521
His report described 36 cases.

435
00:18:56,521 --> 00:18:59,776
Blood, given quickly to a man in shock, could bring

436
00:18:59,776 --> 00:19:01,243
him back from the edge.

437
00:19:01,693 --> 00:19:03,742
It was still new, and still small.

438
00:19:03,742 --> 00:19:06,815
But the idea of a blood bank, blood collected ahead

439
00:19:06,815 --> 00:19:10,249
of time, stored and ready, was born on the Western Front.

440
00:19:10,699 --> 00:19:11,678
Today, the U.S.

441
00:19:11,678 --> 00:19:13,962
military still relies on that idea.

442
00:19:13,962 --> 00:19:15,985
And it still depends on donors.

443
00:19:16,435 --> 00:19:19,858
But in 1918, there was a wound that no amount of

444
00:19:19,858 --> 00:19:20,999
blood could fix.

445
00:19:20,999 --> 00:19:22,426
It came on the wind.

446
00:19:23,026 --> 00:19:23,640
Gas.

447
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:28,912
The German Army first used chlorine on a large scale

448
00:19:28,912 --> 00:19:30,536
at Ypres in April 1915.

449
00:19:30,536 --> 00:19:32,796
Phosgene followed that December.

450
00:19:33,246 --> 00:19:36,597
Then, in July 1917, came mustard gas.

451
00:19:37,047 --> 00:19:39,832
In its first weeks, more than fourteen thousand

452
00:19:39,832 --> 00:19:42,914
British gas casualties reached the casualty clearing

453
00:19:42,914 --> 00:19:43,447
stations.

454
00:19:43,447 --> 00:19:44,870
About five hundred died.

455
00:19:45,320 --> 00:19:46,500
Mustard was different.

456
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:48,271
You might not notice it at first.

457
00:19:48,271 --> 00:19:50,203
Symptoms could take hours to appear.

458
00:19:50,203 --> 00:19:52,940
It soaked through clothing and even leather, and it

459
00:19:52,940 --> 00:19:54,657
lingered on the ground for days.

460
00:19:55,107 --> 00:19:58,117
It caused blistering burns, worst on the eyes and in

461
00:19:58,117 --> 00:19:58,811
the airways.

462
00:19:58,811 --> 00:20:01,647
The National Library of Medicine notes that these

463
00:20:01,647 --> 00:20:04,540
burns healed much more slowly than ordinary burns.

464
00:20:04,540 --> 00:20:06,682
Blisters could last up to four weeks.

465
00:20:07,132 --> 00:20:09,421
Many gassed men were temporarily blinded.

466
00:20:09,421 --> 00:20:12,661
Most of them, later research found, recovered their sight.

467
00:20:13,111 --> 00:20:16,362
On the night of February 26, 1918, German gas

468
00:20:16,362 --> 00:20:19,974
projectors fired on the American First Division in

469
00:20:19,974 --> 00:20:21,563
the Ansauville sector.

470
00:20:22,013 --> 00:20:25,573
Two hundred and twenty-five men were exposed.

471
00:20:25,573 --> 00:20:28,183
85 became casualties. Eight died.

472
00:20:28,633 --> 00:20:30,483
Two of the eight died at once.

473
00:20:30,483 --> 00:20:33,691
Six more died after they reached the field hospital.

474
00:20:34,141 --> 00:20:37,495
General Pershing wrote that the effect of gas was so

475
00:20:37,495 --> 00:20:40,848
deadly to the unprepared that we can never afford to

476
00:20:40,848 --> 00:20:42,203
neglect the question.

477
00:20:42,653 --> 00:20:46,035
The Army took gas seriously enough to build a whole

478
00:20:46,035 --> 00:20:47,229
service around it.

479
00:20:47,229 --> 00:20:50,545
Its Gas Service was set up in France in July 1917.

480
00:20:50,545 --> 00:20:53,927
Of all the Americans gassed, about four in ten were

481
00:20:53,927 --> 00:20:54,922
hit by mustard.

482
00:20:55,372 --> 00:20:57,303
So every American carried a mask.

483
00:20:57,303 --> 00:21:00,170
At first they were British small box respirators,

484
00:21:00,170 --> 00:21:02,394
because American masks were not ready.

485
00:21:02,844 --> 00:21:05,177
The masks worked, but they were miserable.

486
00:21:05,177 --> 00:21:06,844
The straps gave men headaches.

487
00:21:06,844 --> 00:21:08,011
The eyepieces fogged.

488
00:21:08,011 --> 00:21:09,789
Saliva pooled at the mouthpiece.

489
00:21:09,789 --> 00:21:12,511
The Assistant Secretary of War said that the word

490
00:21:12,511 --> 00:21:14,400
discomfort was a weak description.

491
00:21:14,850 --> 00:21:18,159
If you were gassed, the system had a special lane for you.

492
00:21:18,159 --> 00:21:20,954
In the Meuse-Argonne, the First Army had four gas

493
00:21:20,954 --> 00:21:21,525
hospitals.

494
00:21:21,975 --> 00:21:25,024
At one American base hospital, gassed men arrived

495
00:21:25,024 --> 00:21:27,887
with a cross marked in crayon on the forehead.

496
00:21:28,337 --> 00:21:31,794
In March 1918, one Army nurse wrote in her diary

497
00:21:31,794 --> 00:21:35,539
that she received 68 gassed men on her floor, mostly

498
00:21:35,539 --> 00:21:39,285
mustard burns, from a single convoy of about 300 men

499
00:21:39,285 --> 00:21:41,374
of the Forty-Second Division.

500
00:21:41,824 --> 00:21:44,650
Their clothes came off, because the clothes were

501
00:21:44,650 --> 00:21:45,533
still poisoned.

502
00:21:45,533 --> 00:21:46,534
They were washed.

503
00:21:46,534 --> 00:21:49,595
Mobile degassing units could give a man a two-minute

504
00:21:49,595 --> 00:21:50,007
shower.

505
00:21:50,007 --> 00:21:51,244
The eyes were rinsed.

506
00:21:51,244 --> 00:21:53,481
Damaged lungs got bed rest and oxygen.

507
00:21:53,931 --> 00:21:56,475
Here is the strange thing about gas in the First

508
00:21:56,475 --> 00:21:57,005
World War.

509
00:21:57,005 --> 00:21:58,861
It was not usually what killed men.

510
00:21:58,861 --> 00:22:00,345
It was what filled the beds.

511
00:22:00,795 --> 00:22:04,236
About 70,000 Americans were hospitalized for gas.

512
00:22:04,236 --> 00:22:08,240
Nearly a third of all the wounded who reached a hospital.

513
00:22:08,240 --> 00:22:10,417
About 1,200 of them died there.

514
00:22:10,867 --> 00:22:14,814
Gas injuries, as one hospital history put it, had

515
00:22:14,814 --> 00:22:17,472
high morbidity and low mortality.

516
00:22:17,472 --> 00:22:18,680
Many were hurt.

517
00:22:18,680 --> 00:22:20,693
Few, by comparison, died.

518
00:22:21,143 --> 00:22:24,080
In the British Army, later research found, mustard

519
00:22:24,080 --> 00:22:27,076
killed about two and a half of every hundred men it

520
00:22:27,076 --> 00:22:30,189
injured, the lowest death rate of the main war gases.

521
00:22:30,189 --> 00:22:33,243
What it did was take men out of the fight, for weeks

522
00:22:33,243 --> 00:22:33,830
at a time.

523
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:37,911
In June 1918, the Army created the Chemical Warfare

524
00:22:37,911 --> 00:22:38,480
Service.

525
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:42,110
Today, Army medical units still drill in gas masks.

526
00:22:42,560 --> 00:22:45,632
But for the men who were not gassed, the ones with

527
00:22:45,632 --> 00:22:48,766
broken bones and torn muscle and steel inside them,

528
00:22:48,766 --> 00:22:50,917
the field hospital was only a stop.

529
00:22:50,917 --> 00:22:53,497
The real surgery was waiting further back.

530
00:22:54,097 --> 00:22:54,864
The table.

531
00:22:56,464 --> 00:22:59,495
On paper, the evacuation hospitals stood nine to

532
00:22:59,495 --> 00:23:01,453
fifteen miles behind the front.

533
00:23:01,453 --> 00:23:04,611
This was where a wounded man could expect his real

534
00:23:04,611 --> 00:23:05,242
operation.

535
00:23:05,692 --> 00:23:08,141
They were planned for 432 patients.

536
00:23:08,141 --> 00:23:10,450
They were enlarged to a thousand.

537
00:23:10,450 --> 00:23:13,669
Moving one took ninety 3-ton trucks, or thirty

538
00:23:13,669 --> 00:23:14,649
railroad cars.

539
00:23:15,099 --> 00:23:18,750
A surgical team was two surgeons, an anesthetist and

540
00:23:18,750 --> 00:23:19,522
two nurses.

541
00:23:19,522 --> 00:23:23,384
By mid-1918, a hospital could run up to fourteen teams.

542
00:23:23,384 --> 00:23:26,966
An experienced team could do 35 to 40 operations in

543
00:23:26,966 --> 00:23:27,527
a shift.

544
00:23:27,977 --> 00:23:29,666
Teams often worked two tables.

545
00:23:29,666 --> 00:23:32,592
While one patient was on the first, the second table

546
00:23:32,592 --> 00:23:34,844
was cleaned and the next man brought in.

547
00:23:35,294 --> 00:23:38,646
At Belleau Wood in June, Evacuation Hospital Number

548
00:23:38,646 --> 00:23:41,538
Seven had no operating equipment of its own.

549
00:23:41,538 --> 00:23:44,759
Its surgeons did all their operations on the four

550
00:23:44,759 --> 00:23:47,191
tables of Mobile Hospital Number One.

551
00:23:47,641 --> 00:23:49,899
Anesthesia was ether or chloroform.

552
00:23:49,899 --> 00:23:53,254
A British anesthetist, Geoffrey Marshall, found that

553
00:23:53,254 --> 00:23:56,415
both were dangerous for men already in shock, and

554
00:23:56,415 --> 00:23:59,254
pushed for nitrous oxide and oxygen instead.

555
00:23:59,704 --> 00:24:02,034
Needles and syringes were not thrown away.

556
00:24:02,034 --> 00:24:04,863
They were sterilized, re-sharpened, and used again.

557
00:24:05,313 --> 00:24:09,143
In the Meuse-Argonne, Evacuation Hospital No.

558
00:24:09,143 --> 00:24:10,420
6 set a record:

559
00:24:10,420 --> 00:24:14,590
its surgeons operated on 350 men in a single day.

560
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:18,641
At another, surgeons worked 72 hours with no rest.

561
00:24:19,091 --> 00:24:22,064
And during the fighting at Soissons that summer,

562
00:24:22,064 --> 00:24:25,098
about 1,500 patients lay waiting for surgery at a

563
00:24:25,098 --> 00:24:28,071
single field hospital, while the surgeons worked

564
00:24:28,071 --> 00:24:29,929
24-hour shifts for eight days.

565
00:24:30,379 --> 00:24:32,914
What did the surgeon actually do to you?

566
00:24:32,914 --> 00:24:36,590
He fought the infection the only way anyone could in 1918.

567
00:24:36,590 --> 00:24:37,414
With a knife.

568
00:24:37,864 --> 00:24:40,962
The method was called debridement, or wound excision.

569
00:24:40,962 --> 00:24:43,885
The surgeon cut away every piece of dead and dirty

570
00:24:43,885 --> 00:24:46,867
tissue, and every scrap of cloth and dirt, until he

571
00:24:46,867 --> 00:24:48,679
reached healthy, living muscle.

572
00:24:49,129 --> 00:24:51,322
Dead tissue was where gas gangrene grew.

573
00:24:51,322 --> 00:24:53,571
Take it away, and you took away its home.

574
00:24:54,021 --> 00:24:57,200
The method had been revived by a Belgian surgeon,

575
00:24:57,200 --> 00:25:00,184
Antoine Depage, and was written up by surgeons

576
00:25:00,184 --> 00:25:02,779
serving with the British forces in 1915.

577
00:25:02,779 --> 00:25:06,088
By the time the Americans arrived, it was the rule.

578
00:25:06,538 --> 00:25:10,384
And on the British front, by one historian's count,

579
00:25:10,384 --> 00:25:13,853
gas gangrene fell from about five or 6% of all

580
00:25:13,853 --> 00:25:17,473
wounds in 1915 to just over one percent in 1918.

581
00:25:17,923 --> 00:25:21,186
The laboratories fighting it were improvised too.

582
00:25:21,186 --> 00:25:22,517
At Base Hospital No.

583
00:25:22,517 --> 00:25:25,381
28, there was no proper incubator for these

584
00:25:25,381 --> 00:25:28,777
oxygen-hating bacteria, so the staff built one from

585
00:25:28,777 --> 00:25:30,508
a ten-gallon gasoline can.

586
00:25:30,958 --> 00:25:33,696
Then, instead of sewing the wound shut, surgeons

587
00:25:33,696 --> 00:25:36,606
often left it open, and closed it a few days later,

588
00:25:36,606 --> 00:25:37,632
once it was clean.

589
00:25:37,632 --> 00:25:39,914
This was called delayed primary closure.

590
00:25:40,364 --> 00:25:43,240
Some wounds were washed for days with a new

591
00:25:43,240 --> 00:25:46,719
antiseptic, a dilute bleach solution designed by the

592
00:25:46,719 --> 00:25:49,461
chemist Henry Dakin and used by the Nobel

593
00:25:49,461 --> 00:25:51,869
Prize-winning surgeon Alexis Carrel.

594
00:25:51,869 --> 00:25:55,280
A New York Times headline claimed it made infection

595
00:25:55,280 --> 00:25:56,818
impossible. It did not.

596
00:25:56,818 --> 00:25:57,755
But it helped.

597
00:25:58,205 --> 00:26:00,613
Speed decided almost everything.

598
00:26:00,613 --> 00:26:04,451
One American surgeon wrote in 1918 that the wounded

599
00:26:04,451 --> 00:26:08,288
man should arrive at the evacuation hospital within

600
00:26:08,288 --> 00:26:08,965
12 hours.

601
00:26:09,415 --> 00:26:12,592
When men were operated on within 12 hours, he

602
00:26:12,592 --> 00:26:16,052
reported, about 80% of the wounds could be closed

603
00:26:16,052 --> 00:26:18,099
successfully within ten days.

604
00:26:18,549 --> 00:26:21,747
At a British general hospital far behind the lines,

605
00:26:21,747 --> 00:26:25,007
by comparison, he noted that men often arrived about

606
00:26:25,007 --> 00:26:27,201
three days after they were wounded.

607
00:26:27,651 --> 00:26:30,153
The pace never let up until the very end.

608
00:26:30,153 --> 00:26:33,266
The Twelfth Evacuation Hospital, at Royaumeix, took

609
00:26:33,266 --> 00:26:36,378
in twenty-seven hundred patients in fifty-six days.

610
00:26:36,378 --> 00:26:39,552
Wounded men were still arriving after the armistice,

611
00:26:39,552 --> 00:26:42,970
men who could not be reached while the fighting went on.

612
00:26:43,420 --> 00:26:45,335
There was the 12-hour clock again.

613
00:26:45,785 --> 00:26:48,716
And to find steel inside a man, they had a new tool:

614
00:26:48,716 --> 00:26:49,279
the X-ray.

615
00:26:49,729 --> 00:26:51,536
The French had a head start.

616
00:26:51,536 --> 00:26:54,827
Marie Curie fitted out about twenty X-ray vehicles,

617
00:26:54,827 --> 00:26:56,505
the first by October 1914.

618
00:26:56,505 --> 00:27:00,184
Her daughter Irene, seventeen years old, worked with her.

619
00:27:00,634 --> 00:27:02,223
At Base Hospital No.

620
00:27:02,223 --> 00:27:06,353
28, the X-ray department took more than 3,600 X-rays

621
00:27:06,353 --> 00:27:10,246
in 1918, most of them of arms and legs, to locate

622
00:27:10,246 --> 00:27:12,152
fragments and fractures.

623
00:27:12,602 --> 00:27:13,938
There was no shielding.

624
00:27:13,938 --> 00:27:16,785
Doctors who looked through the fluoroscope screen

625
00:27:16,785 --> 00:27:19,573
with their bare hands in the beam later suffered

626
00:27:19,573 --> 00:27:20,502
radiation burns.

627
00:27:20,502 --> 00:27:21,780
The healers paid, too.

628
00:27:22,230 --> 00:27:24,534
And some wounds needed a specialist.

629
00:27:24,534 --> 00:27:27,669
At a British casualty clearing station during the

630
00:27:27,669 --> 00:27:30,676
fighting at Passchendaele in 1917, the American

631
00:27:30,676 --> 00:27:33,747
surgeon Harvey Cushing operated on soldiers with

632
00:27:33,747 --> 00:27:35,027
wounds to the brain.

633
00:27:35,477 --> 00:27:38,589
He drew steel fragments out with a magnetized nail.

634
00:27:38,589 --> 00:27:41,212
Over three months, the death rate among his

635
00:27:41,212 --> 00:27:44,140
brain-wound patients fell from more than half to

636
00:27:44,140 --> 00:27:44,751
under 30%.

637
00:27:45,201 --> 00:27:48,114
A modern review points out he borrowed much of his

638
00:27:48,114 --> 00:27:49,861
method from European surgeons.

639
00:27:49,861 --> 00:27:53,241
But the numbers suggest what practice under fire could do.

640
00:27:53,691 --> 00:27:55,809
Cushing himself admitted as much.

641
00:27:55,809 --> 00:27:58,635
Aside, possibly, from the principle of track

642
00:27:58,635 --> 00:28:01,909
suction, there is nothing original, he wrote, about

643
00:28:01,909 --> 00:28:02,808
his procedure.

644
00:28:03,258 --> 00:28:05,912
Some wounds stayed deadly no matter what.

645
00:28:05,912 --> 00:28:09,148
In British hospitals, even with surgery, more than

646
00:28:09,148 --> 00:28:12,126
half of the men with penetrating wounds of the

647
00:28:12,126 --> 00:28:12,967
abdomen died.

648
00:28:13,417 --> 00:28:16,803
And as the fighting grew, so did the toll inside the

649
00:28:16,803 --> 00:28:17,455
hospitals.

650
00:28:17,455 --> 00:28:20,776
Deaths among battle casualties admitted to hospital

651
00:28:20,776 --> 00:28:24,032
rose from about four or five percent in the summer

652
00:28:24,032 --> 00:28:27,027
of 1918 to more than six and a half percent in

653
00:28:27,027 --> 00:28:28,395
October and November.

654
00:28:28,845 --> 00:28:31,363
Not every wound, though, could be seen on an X-ray.

655
00:28:31,963 --> 00:28:33,189
The invisible wound.

656
00:28:34,789 --> 00:28:36,789
Soldiers called it shell shock.

657
00:28:36,789 --> 00:28:39,885
Men who had no visible injury, but who shook, or

658
00:28:39,885 --> 00:28:42,982
could not speak, or could not stop reliving what

659
00:28:42,982 --> 00:28:43,885
they had seen.

660
00:28:44,335 --> 00:28:44,892
The U.S.

661
00:28:44,892 --> 00:28:46,492
Army rejected the term.

662
00:28:46,492 --> 00:28:49,414
It preferred another phrase: war neurosis.

663
00:28:49,864 --> 00:28:52,871
The man who shaped the American response was a

664
00:28:52,871 --> 00:28:55,028
psychiatrist named Thomas Salmon.

665
00:28:55,028 --> 00:28:58,100
In May 1917 he went to Britain to study how the

666
00:28:58,100 --> 00:28:59,342
British handled it.

667
00:28:59,792 --> 00:29:03,232
At home, Army screening had already rejected about

668
00:29:03,232 --> 00:29:05,502
2% of recruits on mental grounds.

669
00:29:05,952 --> 00:29:07,220
His main conclusion:

670
00:29:07,220 --> 00:29:10,389
treat these men as close to the front as possible.

671
00:29:10,839 --> 00:29:12,808
Each division got a psychiatrist.

672
00:29:12,808 --> 00:29:15,731
A man brought in shaking was given rest, food and

673
00:29:15,731 --> 00:29:18,774
sleep, near his own unit, with the expectation that

674
00:29:18,774 --> 00:29:19,788
he would go back.

675
00:29:20,238 --> 00:29:23,336
At the division level, the psychiatrists sorted men

676
00:29:23,336 --> 00:29:24,369
under six labels:

677
00:29:24,369 --> 00:29:27,041
shell fright, gas fright, hysteria, fatigue,

678
00:29:27,041 --> 00:29:28,621
malingering and cowardice.

679
00:29:28,621 --> 00:29:31,476
About two thirds were kept with their division.

680
00:29:31,926 --> 00:29:35,284
If that was not enough, he went to a base hospital

681
00:29:35,284 --> 00:29:37,097
ward for up to three weeks.

682
00:29:37,097 --> 00:29:40,053
And the most serious cases went to a special

683
00:29:40,053 --> 00:29:42,068
hospital far behind the lines:

684
00:29:42,068 --> 00:29:43,277
Base Hospital 117.

685
00:29:43,727 --> 00:29:46,989
On the Army's psychiatric wards, patients stood by

686
00:29:46,989 --> 00:29:50,185
their beds for inspection at four in the morning.

687
00:29:50,185 --> 00:29:53,578
Nurses were taught that the social atmosphere of the

688
00:29:53,578 --> 00:29:55,078
ward was all important.

689
00:29:55,528 --> 00:29:59,349
Army doctors reported striking results. At St.

690
00:29:59,349 --> 00:30:03,503
Mihiel, 282 men were seen for shell shock, and 225

691
00:30:03,503 --> 00:30:07,075
went back to their units within three days.

692
00:30:07,525 --> 00:30:09,818
Modern historians are more careful.

693
00:30:09,818 --> 00:30:13,160
A 2007 study of one British forward unit found that

694
00:30:13,160 --> 00:30:16,568
fewer than one in five of its men went straight back

695
00:30:16,568 --> 00:30:19,452
to combat, and called the wartime statistics

696
00:30:19,452 --> 00:30:21,155
inaccurate and misleading.

697
00:30:21,605 --> 00:30:25,292
What is not in doubt is how long this wound lasted.

698
00:30:25,292 --> 00:30:28,979
In 1921, more than one in four former servicemen in

699
00:30:28,979 --> 00:30:32,449
hospital were classed as neuropsychiatric cases.

700
00:30:32,899 --> 00:30:36,608
In the Meuse-Argonne alone, the Medical Department

701
00:30:36,608 --> 00:30:39,798
treated more than 2,000 neurological cases,

702
00:30:39,798 --> 00:30:43,730
alongside 72,000 wounded and more than 18,000 gassed.

703
00:30:44,180 --> 00:30:47,212
Some of the men who came home carried this wound for

704
00:30:47,212 --> 00:30:48,903
years after the guns stopped.

705
00:30:49,353 --> 00:30:52,169
There was another, humbler wound of the trenches:

706
00:30:52,169 --> 00:30:52,687
the feet.

707
00:30:52,687 --> 00:30:55,388
Trench foot came from days of cold, wet feet in

708
00:30:55,388 --> 00:30:56,193
leaking boots.

709
00:30:56,643 --> 00:30:59,861
The British recorded more than twenty thousand cases

710
00:30:59,861 --> 00:31:02,893
in the first winter alone, and about seventy-four

711
00:31:02,893 --> 00:31:04,254
thousand over the war.

712
00:31:04,254 --> 00:31:05,739
The cure was prevention:

713
00:31:05,739 --> 00:31:08,772
dry socks, changed several times a day, and whale

714
00:31:08,772 --> 00:31:10,319
oil rubbed into the feet.

715
00:31:10,319 --> 00:31:13,537
In many units, neglecting your feet was a punishable

716
00:31:13,537 --> 00:31:14,032
offense.

717
00:31:14,482 --> 00:31:17,221
And while the armies fought, a different enemy was

718
00:31:17,221 --> 00:31:18,591
already inside the camps.

719
00:31:19,191 --> 00:31:20,189
The other enemy.

720
00:31:21,789 --> 00:31:24,771
In the autumn of 1918, at the same time as the

721
00:31:24,771 --> 00:31:28,077
Meuse-Argonne, the deadliest flu pandemic in modern

722
00:31:28,077 --> 00:31:30,087
history swept through the Army.

723
00:31:30,537 --> 00:31:33,812
According to a study in Public Health Reports, the

724
00:31:33,812 --> 00:31:35,056
journal of the U.S.

725
00:31:35,056 --> 00:31:38,266
Public Health Service, influenza sickened about a

726
00:31:38,266 --> 00:31:41,672
quarter of the entire Army, more than a million men.

727
00:31:41,672 --> 00:31:45,078
Almost thirty thousand died before they ever reached

728
00:31:45,078 --> 00:31:45,537
France.

729
00:31:45,987 --> 00:31:51,174
In France in 1918, 340,000 American soldiers were

730
00:31:51,174 --> 00:31:54,032
hospitalized for influenza.

731
00:31:54,032 --> 00:31:57,948
227,000 were hospitalized for wounds.

732
00:31:58,398 --> 00:32:00,803
The flu filled more beds than the enemy did.

733
00:32:01,253 --> 00:32:04,304
During the Meuse-Argonne, in October, 1,451

734
00:32:04,304 --> 00:32:06,149
Americans died of the flu.

735
00:32:06,149 --> 00:32:09,412
The sick clogged the same evacuation lines the

736
00:32:09,412 --> 00:32:10,477
wounded needed.

737
00:32:10,927 --> 00:32:14,732
By the end of 1918, about 45,000 American soldiers

738
00:32:14,732 --> 00:32:19,070
had died of influenza and the pneumonia that followed it.

739
00:32:19,070 --> 00:32:22,952
Flu and pneumonia killed nearly as many soldiers as

740
00:32:22,952 --> 00:32:24,094
enemy fire did.

741
00:32:24,544 --> 00:32:26,142
Back home it was the same.

742
00:32:26,142 --> 00:32:29,156
At Walter Reed in Washington, the flu caused more

743
00:32:29,156 --> 00:32:32,169
than 2,000 admissions and more than a hundred and

744
00:32:32,169 --> 00:32:32,968
fifty deaths.

745
00:32:33,418 --> 00:32:36,870
A statistician for the War Department put it bluntly

746
00:32:36,870 --> 00:32:37,402
in 1919.

747
00:32:37,402 --> 00:32:41,252
Pneumonia killed more soldiers than were killed in battle.

748
00:32:41,702 --> 00:32:43,527
And it hit the healers hard.

749
00:32:43,527 --> 00:32:46,589
More than 21,000 Army nurses served in the war.

750
00:32:46,589 --> 00:32:49,130
More than 10,000 of them went overseas.

751
00:32:49,580 --> 00:32:52,912
When America entered the war, in April 1917, the

752
00:32:52,912 --> 00:32:56,452
Army Nurse Corps had 400 and three nurses on active

753
00:32:56,452 --> 00:32:59,368
duty and a hundred and seventy in reserve.

754
00:32:59,818 --> 00:33:01,247
Hospitals overflowed.

755
00:33:01,247 --> 00:33:04,652
Base Hospital Twenty, built for five hundred, held

756
00:33:04,652 --> 00:33:08,533
up to two thousand two hundred and seventy-five patients.

757
00:33:08,533 --> 00:33:11,869
Base Hospital Ten took in fourteen hundred in its

758
00:33:11,869 --> 00:33:12,618
first week.

759
00:33:13,068 --> 00:33:16,912
Not one Army nurse was killed by enemy action.

760
00:33:16,912 --> 00:33:20,253
But 272 died of disease, most of them of

761
00:33:20,253 --> 00:33:23,428
tuberculosis, influenza and pneumonia.

762
00:33:23,878 --> 00:33:28,935
In all, the Army Medical Department grew to about

763
00:33:28,935 --> 00:33:34,095
30,500 medical officers, 21,500 nurses and 264,000

764
00:33:34,095 --> 00:33:35,437
enlisted men.

765
00:33:35,887 --> 00:33:37,742
They worked under fire too.

766
00:33:37,742 --> 00:33:40,903
Nurse Beatrice McDonald lost an eye to a shell

767
00:33:40,903 --> 00:33:44,339
splinter at a British casualty clearing station in

768
00:33:44,339 --> 00:33:45,164
August 1917.

769
00:33:45,614 --> 00:33:49,221
Julia Stimson led the nurses of Base Hospital 21 at

770
00:33:49,221 --> 00:33:52,686
Rouen, and by the end of the war she directed the

771
00:33:52,686 --> 00:33:53,535
whole A.E.F.

772
00:33:53,535 --> 00:33:54,666
nursing service.

773
00:33:54,666 --> 00:33:58,344
She later became the first woman in the Army to hold

774
00:33:58,344 --> 00:33:59,617
the rank of major.

775
00:34:00,067 --> 00:34:01,483
Julia Stimson wrote:

776
00:34:01,483 --> 00:34:05,093
one need never tell me that women can't do as much,

777
00:34:05,093 --> 00:34:07,783
stand as much, and be as brave as men.

778
00:34:08,383 --> 00:34:11,554
In a letter home from Paris in April 1918, a week

779
00:34:11,554 --> 00:34:13,560
into a new job, she was honest.

780
00:34:13,560 --> 00:34:16,343
I have been, she wrote, simply floundering.

781
00:34:16,793 --> 00:34:19,486
Army nurses were decorated by three countries.

782
00:34:19,486 --> 00:34:22,354
Twenty-eight received the French Croix de Guerre,

783
00:34:22,354 --> 00:34:25,223
sixty-nine the British Royal Red Cross, and three

784
00:34:25,223 --> 00:34:27,096
the Distinguished Service Cross.

785
00:34:27,546 --> 00:34:29,580
And the Army needed more of them.

786
00:34:29,580 --> 00:34:32,725
In May 1918, it approved an Army School of Nursing.

787
00:34:32,725 --> 00:34:36,239
In five months, it received eleven thousand applications.

788
00:34:36,239 --> 00:34:38,335
Its first dean was Annie Goodrich.

789
00:34:38,785 --> 00:34:42,044
Not every nurse who volunteered was allowed to serve.

790
00:34:42,044 --> 00:34:45,180
Black nurses enrolled in the Red Cross reserve were

791
00:34:45,180 --> 00:34:47,455
not called until after the armistice.

792
00:34:47,455 --> 00:34:50,591
Then, nine of them served at Army camps in Ohio and

793
00:34:50,591 --> 00:34:51,145
Illinois.

794
00:34:51,595 --> 00:34:54,765
Once your wound was cleaned and closed, or your

795
00:34:54,765 --> 00:34:58,340
fever broke, it was time to move again. Further back.

796
00:34:58,340 --> 00:34:59,217
Much further.

797
00:34:59,817 --> 00:35:00,435
Home.

798
00:35:02,035 --> 00:35:05,502
From the evacuation hospitals, the wounded went back

799
00:35:05,502 --> 00:35:06,102
by train.

800
00:35:06,102 --> 00:35:07,835
An ambulance carried four.

801
00:35:07,835 --> 00:35:10,168
A hospital train carried up to 400.

802
00:35:10,618 --> 00:35:13,824
The Army ran its hospital trains like a railroad of

803
00:35:13,824 --> 00:35:14,327
its own.

804
00:35:14,327 --> 00:35:17,534
Ward cars with tiers of stretcher racks, a pharmacy

805
00:35:17,534 --> 00:35:18,728
car, a kitchen car.

806
00:35:19,178 --> 00:35:22,919
During the Meuse-Argonne alone, 408 trainloads

807
00:35:22,919 --> 00:35:25,766
carried more than 151,000 patients.

808
00:35:25,766 --> 00:35:29,995
The Army had only 17 hospital trains of its own, and

809
00:35:29,995 --> 00:35:32,272
borrowed 46 from the French.

810
00:35:32,722 --> 00:35:35,301
Sometimes a train was sidetracked on the way.

811
00:35:35,301 --> 00:35:38,053
The Army's own history admits that some of those

812
00:35:38,053 --> 00:35:39,085
delays cost lives.

813
00:35:39,535 --> 00:35:42,890
At the end of the line were the base hospitals, far

814
00:35:42,890 --> 00:35:44,074
from the fighting.

815
00:35:44,074 --> 00:35:47,298
Base Hospital 28, at Limoges, was about 200 miles

816
00:35:47,298 --> 00:35:48,285
from the front.

817
00:35:48,285 --> 00:35:51,311
Some were built from scratch, row after row of

818
00:35:51,311 --> 00:35:52,167
wooden wards.

819
00:35:52,617 --> 00:35:55,018
Others moved into whatever was there.

820
00:35:55,018 --> 00:35:58,133
At Vichy, the spa town, hotels became part of an

821
00:35:58,133 --> 00:35:59,496
Army hospital center.

822
00:35:59,946 --> 00:36:01,510
Hospitals specialized.

823
00:36:01,510 --> 00:36:04,496
Base Hospital Sixty-Nine at Savenay fitted

824
00:36:04,496 --> 00:36:05,705
artificial limbs.

825
00:36:05,705 --> 00:36:09,331
Base Hospital Fifteen at Chaumont took face wounds.

826
00:36:09,331 --> 00:36:12,175
Base Hospital Sixty-Four took gas cases.

827
00:36:12,175 --> 00:36:15,303
And Base Hospital 117 took the war neuroses.

828
00:36:15,753 --> 00:36:16,841
Base Hospital No.

829
00:36:16,841 --> 00:36:20,168
4, the Cleveland unit that had sailed first, treated

830
00:36:20,168 --> 00:36:23,303
eighty-two thousand, one hundred and seventy-nine

831
00:36:23,303 --> 00:36:24,774
cases in twenty months.

832
00:36:25,224 --> 00:36:28,796
By the end of the war, American hospitals in France

833
00:36:28,796 --> 00:36:30,898
could treat 403,000 bed cases.

834
00:36:30,898 --> 00:36:34,330
Taking in more than a thousand patients and doing

835
00:36:34,330 --> 00:36:37,972
over a hundred operations in a day was, in the words

836
00:36:37,972 --> 00:36:40,564
of one official history, not unusual.

837
00:36:41,014 --> 00:36:43,635
Here, for the first time in weeks, there were clean

838
00:36:43,635 --> 00:36:45,125
sheets, and nurses, and time.

839
00:36:45,575 --> 00:36:48,282
Many wards were simply rows of tents.

840
00:36:48,282 --> 00:36:51,719
Julia Stimson described rows and rows of canvas

841
00:36:51,719 --> 00:36:54,645
tents, each holding about fourteen beds.

842
00:36:55,095 --> 00:36:56,258
And something else.

843
00:36:56,258 --> 00:36:58,340
Women called reconstruction aides.

844
00:36:58,340 --> 00:37:01,218
They massaged stiff limbs, taught exercises and

845
00:37:01,218 --> 00:37:04,525
crafts, and helped men learn to use their hands again.

846
00:37:04,975 --> 00:37:07,856
They often worked 14-hour days, as civilians.

847
00:37:07,856 --> 00:37:11,186
They were granted veteran status only about 60 years

848
00:37:11,186 --> 00:37:11,570
later.

849
00:37:12,020 --> 00:37:15,486
Here is the number that the whole chain was built for.

850
00:37:15,486 --> 00:37:18,823
In the Army's statistical summary, five out of every

851
00:37:18,823 --> 00:37:21,903
six men sent to hospitals because of wounds were

852
00:37:21,903 --> 00:37:23,636
cured and returned to duty.

853
00:37:24,086 --> 00:37:27,293
And of all the wounded, the same summary found that

854
00:37:27,293 --> 00:37:30,436
the number who died of their wounds was only about

855
00:37:30,436 --> 00:37:32,574
6% as large as the number wounded.

856
00:37:33,024 --> 00:37:35,667
For those who could not go back to the war, the

857
00:37:35,667 --> 00:37:36,736
chain ended at sea.

858
00:37:37,186 --> 00:37:40,745
Stretchers were carried aboard ships at the French

859
00:37:40,745 --> 00:37:42,097
ports, in the rain.

860
00:37:42,097 --> 00:37:45,584
One of them was the Navy hospital ship USS Mercy.

861
00:37:45,584 --> 00:37:48,930
Beginning in November 1918, she made four round

862
00:37:48,930 --> 00:37:52,346
trips, and by March 1919 had brought home nearly

863
00:37:52,346 --> 00:37:53,556
2,000 casualties.

864
00:37:54,006 --> 00:37:54,912
Then New York.

865
00:37:54,912 --> 00:37:57,434
The Statue of Liberty through the haze.

866
00:37:57,884 --> 00:38:00,837
The Signal Corps cameras filmed the wounded being

867
00:38:00,837 --> 00:38:03,308
carried down the gangways, past Red Cross

868
00:38:03,308 --> 00:38:06,683
volunteers, and loaded into Army ambulances on the pier.

869
00:38:07,133 --> 00:38:11,028
Many went to Army hospitals in the United States.

870
00:38:11,028 --> 00:38:14,684
Walter Reed General Hospital in Washington had

871
00:38:14,684 --> 00:38:16,910
opened in 1909 with 80 beds.

872
00:38:16,910 --> 00:38:19,533
By the end of 1918, it had 2,500.

873
00:38:19,983 --> 00:38:24,114
In April 1917 it had a hundred and twenty-one patients.

874
00:38:24,114 --> 00:38:28,244
In 1918 alone, it admitted more than thirteen thousand.

875
00:38:28,694 --> 00:38:31,828
Men who had lost a limb began the long work of

876
00:38:31,828 --> 00:38:34,553
learning to live with an artificial one.

877
00:38:34,553 --> 00:38:37,959
According to the National World War I Museum, more

878
00:38:37,959 --> 00:38:40,888
than 4,400 Americans received treatment and

879
00:38:40,888 --> 00:38:42,046
artificial limbs.

880
00:38:42,496 --> 00:38:45,693
When the war began, most American limb makers still

881
00:38:45,693 --> 00:38:48,263
carved artificial legs from wood by hand.

882
00:38:48,263 --> 00:38:49,580
The war changed that.

883
00:38:49,580 --> 00:38:52,463
Walter Reed got its own prosthetic laboratory.

884
00:38:52,913 --> 00:38:54,961
Advertisers saw the market too.

885
00:38:54,961 --> 00:38:58,396
One new artificial arm was sold as the arm that arms

886
00:38:58,396 --> 00:38:59,783
you for work or play.

887
00:39:00,233 --> 00:39:03,520
The Army's reconstruction program ran in thirty-five

888
00:39:03,520 --> 00:39:06,808
general hospitals and eighteen base hospitals in the

889
00:39:06,808 --> 00:39:07,693
United States.

890
00:39:07,693 --> 00:39:10,981
Its stated goal was complete physical and functional

891
00:39:10,981 --> 00:39:11,740
restoration.

892
00:39:12,190 --> 00:39:15,187
Patients at Walter Reed even ran their own newspaper.

893
00:39:15,187 --> 00:39:16,827
They called it The Come-Back.

894
00:39:17,277 --> 00:39:20,903
The war also left Walter Reed with new departments,

895
00:39:20,903 --> 00:39:23,535
for occupational therapy and physical

896
00:39:23,535 --> 00:39:24,602
reconstruction.

897
00:39:24,602 --> 00:39:28,300
As the hospital's command historian later put it, in

898
00:39:28,300 --> 00:39:31,002
World War I, we had a lot of amputees.

899
00:39:31,452 --> 00:39:34,074
In 1921, the Veterans Bureau was created.

900
00:39:34,074 --> 00:39:37,336
The country was learning that the care of a wounded

901
00:39:37,336 --> 00:39:39,830
soldier does not end when the war does.

902
00:39:40,280 --> 00:39:41,822
So what did all this leave us?

903
00:39:42,272 --> 00:39:44,940
Triage, the sorting of the wounded by who needs what

904
00:39:44,940 --> 00:39:45,659
most urgently.

905
00:39:45,659 --> 00:39:47,352
Blood stored before it is needed.

906
00:39:47,352 --> 00:39:49,610
A splint that holds a broken thigh straight.

907
00:39:49,610 --> 00:39:51,457
Clean out the wound, and do it fast.

908
00:39:51,457 --> 00:39:53,715
A chain of care from the front line to home.

909
00:39:54,165 --> 00:39:57,163
Those ideas still run through military medicine.

910
00:39:57,163 --> 00:39:59,973
Today, soldiers testing for the Army's expert

911
00:39:59,973 --> 00:40:03,033
badges, including the Expert Field Medical Badge,

912
00:40:03,033 --> 00:40:05,906
are still tested on their skills in the field.

913
00:40:06,356 --> 00:40:08,623
In 1918, the clock was 12 hours.

914
00:40:08,623 --> 00:40:12,096
The men who fought it were bearers with blistered

915
00:40:12,096 --> 00:40:15,639
hands, drivers in Model T Fords, surgeons on their

916
00:40:15,639 --> 00:40:19,607
third day without sleep, and nurses who never came home.

917
00:40:20,057 --> 00:40:24,372
In the Meuse-Argonne American Cemetery, which holds

918
00:40:24,372 --> 00:40:28,433
more American war dead than any other in Europe,

919
00:40:28,433 --> 00:40:30,802
14,246 Americans are buried.

920
00:40:31,252 --> 00:40:33,975
Most of the 200,000 wounded lived.

921
00:40:34,775 --> 00:40:38,267
Next time on KEPT ALIVE, War Medicine, episode two:

922
00:40:38,267 --> 00:40:41,759
half a century earlier, the American Civil War, and

923
00:40:41,759 --> 00:40:44,225
what it meant to be wounded in 1862.

924
00:40:44,675 --> 00:40:45,815
For education only.

925
00:40:45,815 --> 00:40:47,436
This is not medical advice.

926
00:40:47,436 --> 00:40:50,257
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
